      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "type": "image",
    "version": 1,
    "src": "https://bouldercoloradovoterguide.com/content/images/2026/10/data-src-image-77ce24d9-db92-4fbe-a77c-4ff5e57c9525.png",
    "width": 1035,
    "height": 698,
    "title": "",
    "alt": "Colorado Blue Book diagram showing how Proposition NN raises the TABOR revenue cap for K-12 education",
    "caption": "",
    "cardWidth": "regular",
    "href": ""
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Importantly, increasing the TABOR cap through Proposition NN would allow the state to dedicate more money to K-12 education. Right now, that level is only increased by inflation and growing population. However, costs for education are typically rising faster than inflation, and Colorado’s population growth has begun to stagnate compared to ten years ago.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Here’s another argument in favor from Justin Ogden, a 22-year public schoolteacher in Colorado:",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "The cost of providing a high-quality education continues to rise, but Colorado’s investment in our public schools hasn’t kept up. For decades, Colorado has underfunded public education, earning an “F” grade from research publication Education Weekly. We’re currently underfunding each student by $4,600 and we rank dead last in the nation for teacher wage competitiveness.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-quote",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 8,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "Proposition NN is the best investment for Colorado’s students and schools",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "link",
        "version": 1,
        "rel": null,
        "target": null,
        "title": null,
        "url": "https://www.coloradopolitics.com/2026/08/07/proposition-nn-is-the-best-investment-for-colorados-students-and-schools-guest-column/"
      },
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": " | GUEST COLUMN — Colorado Politics",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-quote",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "One additional note on Proposition NN and its interaction with Proposition 137 (which is the reason I am opposed to Prop 137), per the Colorado Blue Book:",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "How does Proposition NN interact with other measures? Proposition 137 reduces the amount of revenue the state collects that is subject to the TABOR limit. This measure will not affect the Proposition NN limit. However, if the measure passes, the amount of money the state will actually keep and spend under Proposition NN may be reduced or eliminated.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-quote",
    "version": 1
